Full workflow with attention check and SBPS and MSBS data
LimeSurvey cleaning, condition mapping, and merging workflow

Purpose

Create an auditable LimeSurvey dataset from the deidentified 2024 and 2026
exports, harmonize condition-specific ratings, and prepare it for merging with
the PsychoPy participant master. Source exports remain unchanged.

Script

`scripts/limesurvey/limesurvey_processing_with_boredom.R`

This script was created from the user's verified working
`limesurvey_processing(2).R`. The earlier script remains unchanged as a
fallback; additions are restricted to SBPS/MSBS extraction, validation,
scoring, and related output names.

Inputs

- `data/inter/limesurvey/limesurvey_2024_deidentified.csv`
- `data/inter/limesurvey/limesurvey_2026_deidentified.csv`
- `data/processed/psychopy/master/psychopy_master.csv`

Outputs

Checkpoint 1 writes to `data/inter/limesurvey`:

- `limesurvey_participant_checkpoint.csv`: included participants with cleaned
  demographics, IDs, and trial order. Trial ratings are not included yet.
- `limesurvey_participant_audit.csv`: every source row, inclusion status, ID
  correction, PsychoPy match, and order comparison.

Later checkpoints will add:

- `limesurvey_cleaned_with_boredom.csv`: participant metadata, cleaned HB/LB
  effort/boredom/pain ratings, and SBPS/MSBS items and scores.
- `limesurvey_rating_audit.csv`: one row per source rating, retaining its raw
  value, cleaned value, A/B position, mapped HB/LB condition, status, and note.
- `limesurvey_rating_missingness.csv`: available and missing rating counts for
  every included participant and condition.
- `limesurvey_boredom_item_audit.csv`: one row per SBPS/MSBS item, retaining
  source and cleaned values, position/condition/time point, and validity.
- `limesurvey_boredom_missingness.csv`: item completeness and score
  availability for every SBPS/MSBS assessment.
- `limesurvey_attention_counter_audit.csv`: one row per recorded counter value,
  retaining raw/cleaned values, A/B position, mapped condition, validity, and
  cumulative-sequence warnings.
- `limesurvey_attention_counter_missingness.csv`: completeness and sequence
  status for each participant and condition.
- `limesurvey_final_validation_with_boredom.csv`: participant-level structure,
  order, rating, boredom-item, missingness, and demographic checks.
- `data/processed/limesurvey/master/limesurvey_master_with_boredom.csv`:
  approved expanded LimeSurvey master, written only when validation passes.

Checkpoint 1: participant metadata and inclusion

1. Read both deidentified CSV exports as character data.
2. Require the verified year-specific columns for ID, demographics, and order.
   Stop rather than guess if a required column is absent.
3. Convert blank strings and literal `NA` text to missing values.
4. Standardize ID capitalization and remove separators.
5. Apply confirmed ID corrections:
   - `DO11ÜB30` -> `DO11UB30`
   - `DI16ÜB31` -> `DI16UB31`
   - incomplete ID `AN` -> `AN07IE11`
6. Retain both the original and standardized gender values. Standardized values
   are `male`, `female`, and `diverse`.
7. Validate the ID pattern: two letters, two digits, two letters, two digits.
8. Match IDs against the approved PsychoPy master. Obvious tests, pilots,
   invalid IDs, and rows without matching PsychoPy data are excluded from the
   checkpoint but retained in the audit with a reason.
9. Compare LimeSurvey order with PsychoPy `trial_order`. PsychoPy is
   authoritative when the two disagree.
10. Stop before writing the checkpoint if an included ID occurs more than once.

Confirmed participant decisions

- `JU20ÜC09`: LimeSurvey says `HB_LB`, while PsychoPy says `LB_HB`. Retain
  PsychoPy order `LB_HB` and flag the disagreement.
- `BG22OE24`: measurement stopped because the participant became unwell.
  Retain available data and preserve missing final ratings as NA.
- `KA14RE15`: values of 99 mean missing because the survey site crashed.
  Convert them to NA during rating cleaning and document the conversion.
- `DO13UE03`: parts of the wider measurement failed. Retain available
  LimeSurvey and PsychoPy information; preserve missing ratings as NA. Later
  eligibility decisions occur only after all datasets are merged.

Checkpoint 2: ratings

`IMPLEMENTED`

1. Extract effort, boredom, and pain for baseline and trials 01-04 in Blocks A
   and B from both language-specific schemas.
2. Convert ratings to numeric. Accept only 0-10; turn 99 and any other invalid
   value into NA while retaining the original value in the audit.
3. Interpret A and B as measurement positions:
   - `LB_HB`: A = LB and B = HB.
   - `HB_LB`: A = HB and B = LB.
4. Reshape to intelligible condition columns such as
   `effort_LB_baseline`, `effort_LB_01`, ..., `pain_HB_04`.
5. Record participant-, condition-, trial-, and measure-specific missingness.
6. Write one clean participant row containing 30 rating columns:
   two conditions x three measures x baseline/trials 01-04.

Rating validity and missingness

- The valid rating range is 0-10 inclusive.
- Blank values and literal `NA` are retained as NA.
- Non-numeric values and numeric values outside 0-10 are converted to NA and
  marked in `limesurvey_rating_audit.csv`.
- A missing value is never converted to zero.
- `KA14RE15`: all recorded values of 99 are converted to NA and annotated as
  missing after a LimeSurvey site crash.
- `BG22OE24`: the three missing final B-position ratings are retained as NA and
  annotated as resulting from the participant becoming unwell.
- `DO13UE03`: unavailable B-position ratings are retained as NA and annotated.

Checkpoint 2b: SBPS/MSBS item extraction

`IMPLEMENTED`

1. Extract the eight SBPS trait-boredom items assessed once per participant.
2. Extract the eight selected MSBS state-boredom items at A baseline, A post,
   B baseline, and B post.
3. Use the 2024 item identifiers directly:
   - SBPS: `01-08`.
   - MSBS: `01`, `03`, `09`, `10`, `22`, `23`, `24`, `28`.
4. For 2026, use the verified corresponding German question blocks. Confirm
   that the four repeated MSBS blocks contain the same questions in the same
   order after removing LimeSurvey's `...NN` header suffixes. Stop if the
   structure differs.
5. Parse the leading numeric value from responses such as
   `1 - stimme überhaupt nicht zu`; explanatory text is not retained in the
   cleaned master but remains in the item audit.
6. Accept only integer-equivalent values from 1 through 7. Missing values stay
   NA; non-numeric or out-of-range values become NA and block approval.
7. Map MSBS A/B to HB/LB using the same PsychoPy-authoritative `trial_order`
   used for the effort/boredom/pain ratings.
8. Name item variables as:
   - `sbps_01` through `sbps_08`.
   - `msbs_[HB/LB]_[baseline/post]_[item]`.

SBPS/MSBS scores

- Items are retained unchanged on their observed 1-7 direction; no reverse
  scoring is applied.
- Scores are complete-case summaries. No missing item is imputed.
- `*_n_valid` records the number of usable items, from 0 to 8.
- `*_score_sum` is calculated only when all eight items are valid; its possible
  range is 8-56.
- `*_score_mean` is calculated only when all eight items are valid; its possible
  range is 1-7.
- One SBPS score is created per participant.
- Four MSBS scores are created: HB baseline, HB post, LB baseline, and LB post.
- SBPS uses consistently keyed items, so reverse scoring is not required.
- The score variables are transparent derived summaries; the item-level data
  remain the authoritative extracted responses.
- Complete-case sum/mean scoring is a project processing rule chosen to avoid
  undocumented imputation. It should not be interpreted as a published
  missing-item rule or diagnostic threshold.

Documented boredom-scale missingness

- `DO13UE03`: all eight B-post MSBS responses are absent following the
  documented measurement failure. Because this participant's order is
  `LB_HB`, these are `msbs_HB_post_*`. Retain item and score values as NA and
  record `DOCUMENTED_MISSING`.
- No other included participant had missing or invalid SBPS/MSBS values during
  source inspection. Any additional case produces `REVIEW_REQUIRED`.

Checkpoint 2c: attention counter

`IMPLEMENTED`

1. Extract the cumulative clicker-counter readings recorded after trials 01-04
   in measurement positions A and B.
2. Use the exact 2024 columns `countA1`, `countA2`, `countA3`, `countApos`,
   `countB1`, `countB2`, `countB3`, and `countBpos`.
3. Use the exact 2026 columns `counts...40`, `counts...44`, `counts...48`,
   `counts...52`, `counts...77`, `counts...81`, `counts...85`, and
   `counts...89`.
4. Map A/B to HB/LB using the PsychoPy-authoritative `trial_order`.
5. Store the values as `attention_counter_[HB/LB]_[01-04]`.
6. Preserve cumulative readings as recorded. Do not calculate per-trial
   differences in this extraction step.
7. Accept zero or positive whole numbers. Missing, non-numeric, negative, or
   non-integer values become NA; unexpected invalid/missing data require review.
8. Treat `KA14RE15` values of 99 as documented missing data caused by the
   survey-site crash. Do not treat 99 globally as missing for other IDs.
9. Retain the four absent `DO13UE03` HB counter readings as documented NA.
10. Check whether available counter readings decrease within a condition.
    Decreases are warnings only because the source cannot distinguish counter
    reset, entry error, or an interrupted measurement.

Known counter sequence warnings

- `BG22OE24`, Block B: `5, 9, 12, 0`. The participant became unwell and the
  measurement was stopped. Retain the recorded zero pending manual review.
- `AN06AN18`, Block A: `7, 14, 24, 8`. Retain the recorded values pending
  manual review.

Checkpoint 3: final LimeSurvey validation

`IMPLEMENTED`

1. Confirm one LimeSurvey row per included ID.
2. Confirm every retained rating is numeric and within 0-10.
3. Confirm each approved PsychoPy ID has one matching LimeSurvey row and the
   same authoritative trial order.
4. Confirm exactly 30 effort/boredom/pain rating cells and 40 SBPS/MSBS item
   cells per participant.
5. Accept only the documented missing-rating patterns for `BG22OE24`,
   `KA14RE15`, and `DO13UE03`. Any additional missing rating produces
   `REVIEW_REQUIRED`.
6. Flag missing or broadly implausible demographics for review without
   changing their values. Review ranges are age 0-120 years, weight 20-300 kg,
   and height 100-250 cm.
7. Always write the final validation report. Write the processed LimeSurvey
   master only when no row is marked `REVIEW_REQUIRED`.
8. Require all cleaned SBPS/MSBS responses to be in 1-7 and permit only the
   documented `DO13UE03` MSBS missingness pattern.
9. Confirm exactly eight attention-counter cells per participant and permit
   only the documented `KA14RE15` and `DO13UE03` missing values. Sequence
   decreases generate `VALID_WITH_WARNING` rather than automatic correction.

